<Base
  title="Manifesto"
  description="Five principles I work by. They explain how I make decisions, what I'll defend, and what I won't ship."
>
  <main>
    <h1>MANIFESTO</h1>
    <p>
      Five principles I work by. They explain how I make decisions,
      what I'll defend, and what I won't ship.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>01. Every decision has an author.</h2>
      <p>
        If no one will defend it, it shouldn't ship. Every product is built on a few
        thousand small decisions: what the cancel button says, where the password
        strength meter sits, whether the empty state is funny or quiet. Most of those
        get made by accident. I make them on purpose. Settings are unfinished
        decisions. "Make it customizable" is what designers say when they
        couldn't decide.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>02. The brief is wrong until proven right.</h2>
      <p>
        A brief is the founder's first guess at the problem. Sometimes it's right.
        Often it isn't. So the work starts with checking it. If you hand me a brief
        and I do exactly what it says, I'm not designing. I'm typing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>03. Make the call, then check it.</h2>
      <p>
        Research sharpens a decision. It doesn't replace one. Most of the
        patterns aren't new. I make the call from what I've seen, then go
        check it where I'm unsure. Not the other way around.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>04. Character is not a layer.</h2>
      <p>
        A product has one voice or none. The voice shows up in places people
        don't think of as design: the wording on a 404, the timing of a
        toast notification, what your invoice email feels like. If those don't
        match the rest of the product, the product doesn't have a voice.
        It has a styleguide.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>05. The safe version is already taken.</h2>
      <p>
        Every category has its safe version, and it's already taken. The banking
        app in navy blue. The pharmacy site that opens on a sale. Building
        another one puts you second in a line someone else started. You should
        be able to tell whose product it is without seeing the logo.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr />

    <p>If this sounds like how you want to build —</p>
    <a href="/#review">Send a link, get a video review →</a>
  </main>
</Base>
